note: regression test -- syntax keywords like add/count/find/split/replace/match/in/by/not/global must
note: be usable as variable, function, parameter and loop-variable names: declared, then read back.
note: Previously the places that declare or reference a name only accepted
note: TokenType::IDENTIFIER, so using any of these names made parsing
note: fail outright.

set number add to 5
set number count to 10
set number in to 1
set number by to 2
set number global to 3
set number not to 4
set number to to 6
change add to add + 1
print(add)
print(count)
print(in)
print(by)
print(global)
print(not)
print(to)

note: the same name can also be reused as a function (variables and functions have separate namespaces)
set returnable func add(x, y) do:
    return x + y
end
print(add(1, 2))

note: also usable as a loop variable name
loop repeat add to 1 ~ 3 do:
    change count to count + add
end
print(count)

note: match/find/replace/split/count have their own expression syntax,
note: but are treated as plain identifiers when that syntax does not follow.
set str replace to "R"
set str split to "S"
set str find to "F"
set str match to "M"
print(replace)
print(split)
print(find)
print(match)

note: and the real syntax must still work.
use DLC:string
print(split "a,b,c" by ",")
print(find "world" from "hello world")
print(replace "world" in "hello world" to "there")
print(count "a" in "banana")

note: the "is not" comparison must not conflict with using `not` as a variable.
set boolean flag to true
if flag is not false do:
    print("is-not still works")
end
